1
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:04,619
If you are watching this,
then you know very well...

2
00:00:04,699 --> 00:00:06,220
that the Observers have invaded.

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:08,219
Previously on Fringe:

4
00:00:08,299 --> 00:00:12,019
You can't go back to Harvard. We can't
get in. It was taken over by Observers.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,539
Well, that's not a problem. Tunnels.

6
00:00:14,619 --> 00:00:19,020
Two-and-one-quarter miles of tunnels that
house the steam pipes that heat Harvard.

7
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:20,860
I must have ambered it.

8
00:00:20,939 --> 00:00:24,779
My Betamax recorder.
I don't think I wrote anything down.

9
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:26,819
I think I was making a video.

10
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:31,819
This plan will restore us
and rid the world of the Observers.

11
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:34,620
You must recover each tape
and follow the instructions...

12
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,380
to retrieve all the parts of the plan.
You are humanity's only hope.

13
00:00:48,219 --> 00:00:49,899
Slowly.

14
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,779
Suddenly you have all the finesse
of a butcher.

15
00:00:52,859 --> 00:00:55,340
Walter, do you want me to do this or not?

16
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:57,579
This work requires a surgeon's touch.

17
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:02,139
I'm more than happy to let you take over.

18
00:01:02,899 --> 00:01:04,619
Fine. Proceed.

19
00:01:04,699 --> 00:01:06,419
But it's on your shoulders.

20
00:01:20,539 --> 00:01:22,140
Not even warped from the heat.

21
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,419
- Not bad for a butcher.
- Walter?

22
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,539
That's more fuel for the van.

23
00:01:27,619 --> 00:01:30,259
- I have tape number one.
- Have you watched it?

24
00:01:30,339 --> 00:01:33,100
No. I've only just liberated it.

25
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:36,140
Shall we have a look?

26
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:57,660
I had a prescription, it was medicinal.

27
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:02,819
Welcome to Tape 3 of my plan...

28
00:02:02,899 --> 00:02:05,500
- What about 1 and 2?
- To defeat the Observers...

29
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:08,340
Walter, you stored
the tapes out of order.

30
00:02:08,419 --> 00:02:09,419
Shocker.

31
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:11,460
Videotapes must be retrieved.

32
00:02:11,539 --> 00:02:17,500
This tape will explain one of the most
crucial elements that you will need.

33
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:21,739
You need to find your way
to Pennsylvania.

34
00:02:21,819 --> 00:02:24,299
Latitude and longitude coordinates...

35
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:27,979
49 degrees, 20 minutes,
2 seconds north...

36
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:32,299
79 degrees, 12 minutes
and 32 seconds west.

37
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:34,900
When you arrive...

38
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:39,340
- Really?
- See if you can rewind the coordinates.

39
00:02:39,419 --> 00:02:43,220
Forty-one degrees, 20 minutes,
2 seconds north...

40
00:02:43,299 --> 00:02:47,220
79 degrees, 12
minutes, 32 seconds west.

41
00:02:47,299 --> 00:02:49,259
- Are you sure?
- She's sure.

42
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:51,380
Walter.

43
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:55,539
Do you recognize the location?

44
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:59,660
I'm sorry. If I've been there,
it's been wiped from my mind...

45
00:02:59,739 --> 00:03:03,819
along with the other pieces of my plan
to beat the Observers.

46
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:05,579
Hm.

47
00:03:06,299 --> 00:03:09,539
The entire area is undeveloped.
There's nothing around it for miles.

48
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:11,579
How are roads
in and out of Pennsylvania?

49
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,139
Think we can get there safely?

50
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:15,019
We'd need to avoid checkpoints.

51
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:17,979
Long way, considering we don't know
what we're looking for.

52
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:19,620
Or if it's still even there.

53
00:03:19,699 --> 00:03:21,620
- Whatever it is.
- Hey, Walter.

54
00:03:21,699 --> 00:03:24,460
You think we might be able
to jog your memory if we just...?

55
00:03:25,979 --> 00:03:27,979
I don't think I should travel.

56
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:31,539
My feet are swollen from standing
in front of the amber all day.

57
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:35,940
I'll stay here for a bit and regroup.

58
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:38,819
What?

59
00:03:59,819 --> 00:04:01,100
Walter, are you all right?

60
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:03,419
I'm fairly certain I
have a blood clot...

61
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,060
because somebody wouldn't stop
at Scranton to let me stretch my legs.

62
00:04:07,139 --> 00:04:10,739
Walter, we're fugitives. We can't stop
every five minutes to keep you limber.

63
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:17,900
This is it?

64
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:22,059
Yes. This is exactly
where we're supposed to be.

65
00:04:24,059 --> 00:04:26,379
So, what were you doing out here
21 years ago?

66
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:56,619
Is that what we're out here to find?

67
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:05,140
I'm telling you, it was
a wicked tree dwarf.

68
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:09,179
And I'm telling you it was a kid.
I saw him. He looked right at me.

69
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:14,619
You know, I used to imagine this.
Going on a mission with you.

70
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:16,939
You have no idea how many battles
we won together.

71
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:21,100
You know, it's a lot to live up to.

72
00:05:21,179 --> 00:05:25,260
No. You're even more than I imagined.

73
00:05:42,739 --> 00:05:44,780
Definitely not dwarves.

74
00:06:07,859 --> 00:06:10,619
Don't touch them.
It could be communicable.

75
00:06:38,419 --> 00:06:40,100
Stop right there.

76
00:07:12,499 --> 00:07:15,299
Dr. Walter Bishop.

77
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:24,059
The three of you.

78
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:25,820
You haven't aged a day.

79
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,100
You're supposed to be dead
a long time ago.

80
00:07:36,939 --> 00:07:40,100
We were trapped in amber for 21 years.

81
00:07:42,859 --> 00:07:44,700
My name is Edwin.

82
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:49,379
You were exposed when you got here.

83
00:08:01,979 --> 00:08:05,020
I'm supposed to
retrieve something here, Edwin.

84
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:06,700
Something very important.

85
00:08:09,179 --> 00:08:13,820
You seem to know me.
Have we met here before?

86
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,059
No.

87
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,619
At least not in the way that you think.

88
00:08:43,259 --> 00:08:46,060
It's electromagnetic discharge.

89
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:54,540
What is this place?

90
00:08:55,820 --> 00:08:58,259
- You're looking at history.
- Whose?

91
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:02,779
Ours. Yours.

92
00:09:02,859 --> 00:09:04,700
Mankind's.

93
00:09:06,019 --> 00:09:08,940
These are data cubes.

94
00:09:09,019 --> 00:09:12,700
Stored on them are accounts
of every major historical event...

95
00:09:12,779 --> 00:09:15,300
from the day of the invasion onward.

96
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:24,100
- You have files on us.
- Of course. You're relevant.

97
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:27,259
We gather important information from
around the world and record it.

98
00:09:28,499 --> 00:09:30,940
Hm. You have a healthy lore about you.

99
00:09:32,139 --> 00:09:35,019
My son, River, is something of an expert
on the Fringe Division.

100
00:09:40,859 --> 00:09:44,580
We were a group of refugees
from the cities.

101
00:09:44,659 --> 00:09:47,899
My father started this historical record.

102
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:53,340
He believed our history, like that
of other conquered races before us...

103
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:55,180
would be rewritten by our oppressors.

104
00:09:56,619 --> 00:09:59,619
Someone has to survive
to tell mankind's story.

105
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:07,979
What happened to all of you? Your skin.
You said we've already been exposed.

106
00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:10,540
We noticed it after we settled.

107
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:14,220
Small patches at first.
And then, over time, it spread.

108
00:10:14,779 --> 00:10:18,300
Something to do with this area.
We thought it was the drinking water...

109
00:10:18,379 --> 00:10:21,220
- or the soil.
- Why didn't you evacuate?

110
00:10:25,619 --> 00:10:27,259
What we do here is important.

111
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:31,899
Even after we became afflicted,
we couldn't leave. It's all here.

112
00:10:31,979 --> 00:10:34,139
Everything. We can't move it.

113
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:35,779
We can't travel the way we look.

114
00:10:35,859 --> 00:10:38,659
We don't wanna draw attention.

115
00:10:40,460 --> 00:10:43,420
We just wanna be left alone
to record history.

116
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:46,300
No one wants to come here...

117
00:10:46,379 --> 00:10:47,899
which is perfect for us.

118
00:10:47,979 --> 00:10:50,460
You're the first in a very long time.

119
00:10:50,779 --> 00:10:53,340
But you and your team should leave.

120
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:55,739
Unless you wanna look like us.

121
00:10:57,019 --> 00:10:58,460
We can't.

122
00:10:59,220 --> 00:11:01,859
There was a plan to defeat the invaders.

123
00:11:01,940 --> 00:11:05,739
I mean, there is a plan
to defeat the invaders.

124
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:08,460
And I believe it will be successful.

125
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:13,379
But I don't remember it.

126
00:11:13,779 --> 00:11:17,379
But I know that I am
supposed to get something from here.

127
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:19,940
I've been here before. I must have.

128
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:24,220
Search Bishop, Walter.

129
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:32,379
If you've ever set foot in this camp
before, it would be documented.

130
00:11:36,499 --> 00:11:37,739
Hi.

131
00:11:39,659 --> 00:11:40,659
Hi.

132
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:43,899
What's your name?

133
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,300
River.

134
00:11:51,859 --> 00:11:53,540
Wow, where did these come from?

135
00:11:54,899 --> 00:11:55,899
I made them.

136
00:11:56,899 --> 00:11:58,139
Can you sign them?

137
00:12:01,779 --> 00:12:04,379
- These are really good.
- Yeah.

138
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:08,540
You fought them when they came.

139
00:12:08,619 --> 00:12:10,180
You didn't hide, like we did.

140
00:12:10,259 --> 00:12:12,220
You're heroes.

141
00:12:13,499 --> 00:12:15,580
I have a lot of different editions...

142
00:12:15,659 --> 00:12:17,659
from stories I've heard.

143
00:12:17,739 --> 00:12:21,580
A lot of them I even made up.
I wish I was around during your time.

144
00:12:21,659 --> 00:12:23,979
When there were real heroes.

145
00:12:24,859 --> 00:12:28,220
Well, kiddo, you're my hero
for giving me a nice, strong jawline.

146
00:12:37,580 --> 00:12:40,019
Minutes and 32 seconds west.

147
00:12:40,100 --> 00:12:43,659
Now, when you arrive
at the designated place...

148
00:12:43,739 --> 00:12:46,779
you will need to locate a...

149
00:12:51,379 --> 00:12:56,540
you arrive at the designated place,
you will need to locate a...

150
00:13:11,499 --> 00:13:15,060
You will need to locate a...

151
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:19,859
To locate a mine.

152
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:23,619
Locate a mine.

153
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:37,619
- Hello?
- Olivia, I need to speak to Walter.

154
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:38,700
Sure.

155
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:40,979
Walter.

156
00:13:41,060 --> 00:13:44,100
- It's Astrid.
- Yes. What is it?

157
00:13:44,180 --> 00:13:46,460
- Did you find anything in the archive?
- Nothing.

158
00:13:46,540 --> 00:13:49,340
There is absolutely no record
of me being here whatsoever.

159
00:13:49,420 --> 00:13:50,820
Okay. Well, I found something.

160
00:13:51,139 --> 00:13:53,019
Walter, is there a mine where you are?

161
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:56,700
Mime. The only mime that I know of
is Marcel Marceau.

162
00:13:56,779 --> 00:13:59,580
- What's he got to do with any of this?
- No, a mine.

163
00:13:59,659 --> 00:14:02,659
You said "mine," M-I-N-E, on the tape.

164
00:14:02,739 --> 00:14:05,180
Whatever you're supposed to find,
it's in a mine.

165
00:14:05,739 --> 00:14:09,979
- Do you have a mine, M-I-N-E, here?
- Yes.

166
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:11,700
It was a gold mine.

167
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:31,139
These men have something to report.

168
00:14:41,899 --> 00:14:43,019
Where?

169
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:44,580
Rural Pennsylvania.

170
00:15:04,340 --> 00:15:07,259
In 1923, a mine collapsed in Peru.

171
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:11,139
The trapped survivors were forced
to nourish themselves on the dead.

172
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:12,340
Thanks for that, Walter.

173
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:14,300
But I'm sure that
won't be the case here.

174
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:19,739
Finding gold in an abandoned
gold mine is not gonna be easy.

175
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:23,779
I don't know that it's gold.
Whatever it is, it's in here.

176
00:15:23,859 --> 00:15:25,340
I'll climb down.

177
00:15:29,979 --> 00:15:31,580
That secure?

178
00:15:33,739 --> 00:15:36,580
There's something on the other end.
Can you give me a hand?

179
00:15:36,659 --> 00:15:37,820
Yep.

180
00:15:39,499 --> 00:15:42,340
People have been coming
in and out of this mine for 21 years.

181
00:15:42,420 --> 00:15:44,540
Doubt it's just gonna be waiting for us.

182
00:15:44,619 --> 00:15:46,460
Nothing is that easy.

183
00:15:49,259 --> 00:15:51,019
Oh, oh, oh. Snagged on something.

184
00:16:06,899 --> 00:16:09,180
Looks like he was trying
to climb back up.

185
00:16:12,139 --> 00:16:14,940
Same bark-like growths
as on the people here.

186
00:16:15,019 --> 00:16:17,979
- But worse.
- I assumed what afflicted these people...

187
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:20,100
was some sort of fungus,
but I was wrong.

188
00:16:20,779 --> 00:16:23,259
These tissues show signs of calcification.

189
00:16:24,940 --> 00:16:29,100
Perhaps whatever was causing
the condition of the people in the camp...

190
00:16:29,180 --> 00:16:30,779
is emanating from this mine.

191
00:16:30,859 --> 00:16:36,739
The closer one gets to the source,
the more intense the reaction is.

192
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:40,379
No one can survive down there.
Until I've conducted an analysis...

193
00:16:40,460 --> 00:16:42,739
we have to turn back.

194
00:16:49,259 --> 00:16:51,739
- This is Anil.
- I have something for you.

195
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:53,340
Something important.

196
00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:56,659
Etta and her team have been located.

197
00:16:58,259 --> 00:16:59,779
Thank you.

198
00:17:00,499 --> 00:17:02,979
Is anybody else starving?

199
00:17:03,499 --> 00:17:04,899
Here.

200
00:17:08,980 --> 00:17:10,820
- What's that?
- It's an apple.

201
00:17:11,619 --> 00:17:13,219
No, thank you.

202
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:14,740
Yum-yum.

203
00:17:19,419 --> 00:17:23,340
Do you remember that amazing apple pie
we had just after the invasion?

204
00:17:24,219 --> 00:17:25,500
We were in Boston...

205
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:28,340
someone had told us
about the shelter for lost children.

206
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:32,780
We were looking for a place to eat
and we found that dingy little restaurant.

207
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:37,020
The cook was a mad genius.
What was that guy's name?

208
00:17:38,419 --> 00:17:39,659
I don't know.

209
00:17:41,179 --> 00:17:42,980
You don't remember?

210
00:17:44,659 --> 00:17:45,899
Yep.

211
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:49,179
Well, that's a first.

212
00:17:50,100 --> 00:17:53,859
I don't remember. It was the name
of the restaurant. Started with a D.

213
00:17:53,939 --> 00:17:58,139
It was like, Dante's or Dominic's
or something like that.

214
00:17:58,219 --> 00:18:01,699
- You really don't remember?
- No, I really don't remember.

215
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:06,619
The air sample is ready.

216
00:18:10,219 --> 00:18:11,540
Hm.

217
00:18:14,139 --> 00:18:16,459
I think I know what happened here.

218
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:21,699
This man died of asphyxiation.

219
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,820
Every pore on his body
is completely sealed.

220
00:18:25,899 --> 00:18:29,379
His body was trying to protect itself
against the airborne corrosive.

221
00:18:29,459 --> 00:18:31,899
Somehow,
its pH has been radically altered.

222
00:18:32,859 --> 00:18:36,419
Apparently it gets much worse
the deeper one goes into the mine.

223
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:40,939
- What we're looking for cause this?
- Quite unlikely. If I had to theorize...

224
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:43,219
it's high levels of
CO2 and other gases...

225
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:45,619
introduced to the atmosphere
by the Observers.

226
00:18:45,699 --> 00:18:47,899
From those machines in Central Park.

227
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,899
How would that affect Edwin
and his people?

228
00:18:51,659 --> 00:18:54,020
Well, they're far enough away
from the source...

229
00:18:54,100 --> 00:18:55,939
receiving only incremental doses.

230
00:18:56,020 --> 00:19:01,980
However, prolonged exposure
has created a drastic immune response.

231
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:06,540
Well, imagine an extreme form
of psoriasis.

232
00:19:06,619 --> 00:19:10,459
Not as extreme as our unfortunate miner
friend here.

233
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:15,459
Dr. Bishop. I've found an entry
I think you'll wanna see.

234
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:18,659
Five weeks after the invasion...

235
00:19:18,740 --> 00:19:20,740
a man came,
went down into the mine...

236
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:23,419
and gathered several rocks,
it says with a reddish hue.

237
00:19:24,459 --> 00:19:27,859
- Was this man called September?
- No.

238
00:19:27,939 --> 00:19:31,899
My father has it recorded as a man,
late 30s, dark hair. His name was Donald.

239
00:19:34,179 --> 00:19:36,699
Says he was waiting for a scientist
from Boston.

240
00:19:36,780 --> 00:19:39,859
- I was thinking perhaps that was you?
- I don't know any Donald.

241
00:19:39,939 --> 00:19:41,659
Well, maybe you just don't remember.

242
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:45,060
If he was part of the plan you lost,
then it stands to reason...

243
00:19:45,139 --> 00:19:47,300
No, no. I don't understand.

244
00:19:47,379 --> 00:19:50,300
Why would you record this,
a man collecting rocks?

245
00:19:50,379 --> 00:19:51,379
Why is it important?

246
00:19:52,020 --> 00:19:54,260
It was recorded
because of what happened to him.

247
00:19:57,340 --> 00:20:00,540
He was taken away
against his will by two invaders.

248
00:20:00,980 --> 00:20:03,179
So they weren't happy
that he'd taken those rocks.

249
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:05,899
Why the hell would I want rocks?

250
00:20:10,859 --> 00:20:11,899
Your hand, Walter.

251
00:20:14,379 --> 00:20:16,379
It's on your skin.

252
00:20:23,340 --> 00:20:24,540
Yours too.

253
00:20:42,820 --> 00:20:45,659
We're lucky.
It hasn't rooted into the dermis yet.

254
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:47,500
But I'm afraid that won't last.

255
00:20:49,219 --> 00:20:51,820
Okay, well, how long
until it isn't removable?

256
00:20:53,260 --> 00:20:54,260
Until it isn't.

257
00:20:55,820 --> 00:20:57,580
Dr. Bishop.

258
00:20:57,659 --> 00:20:59,340
Good, good, good. Excellent, excellent.

259
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:02,100
Over here. Thank you very much.

260
00:21:02,179 --> 00:21:07,060
I am going to build a suit
that will allow us to go into the mine.

261
00:21:08,419 --> 00:21:09,580
According to the arm...

262
00:21:09,659 --> 00:21:12,939
we'd need skin 300 layers
thicker than a rhino to enter the mine.

263
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:18,379
So I'm trying to ascertain a metal or
an alloy we can use for the suit's lining.

264
00:21:18,459 --> 00:21:21,260
And in the meantime,
I need you to go back to the van.

265
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:23,780
- Because I need the weather stripping.
- Sure.

266
00:21:28,859 --> 00:21:31,740
- Hey, Anil?
- I got a call from one of our operatives.

267
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:33,780
The Loyalists are tracking you.

268
00:21:33,859 --> 00:21:34,899
You've got to move.

269
00:21:41,179 --> 00:21:43,179
Did I upset you?

270
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:48,780
Earlier. When I was talking about the
restaurant. Seemed like you were upset.

271
00:21:49,379 --> 00:21:51,899
I know you don't forget names.

272
00:21:52,659 --> 00:21:53,740
Donovan's.

273
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:57,939
That was the name of the restaurant.

274
00:21:59,100 --> 00:22:01,419
It's not that I can't remember.

275
00:22:02,179 --> 00:22:04,100
It's that I can't forget.

276
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:09,260
Remember when we couldn't find her
that day...

277
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:11,300
you drew a plan on a napkin?

278
00:22:12,699 --> 00:22:13,820
Yeah, of course I do.

279
00:22:17,219 --> 00:22:19,379
You were talking about
going up to Maine...

280
00:22:19,459 --> 00:22:22,540
to this refugee camp
that you'd heard about.

281
00:22:22,939 --> 00:22:28,219
And I was looking at you and nodding,
in my head I was thinking, "I can't go."

282
00:22:33,060 --> 00:22:37,419
Behind you, you couldn't see it, there
was a wall of Missing Person flyers.

283
00:22:40,740 --> 00:22:43,179
You know, with every day...

284
00:22:43,260 --> 00:22:47,020
the odds of finding our little girl the
way we wanted to were getting worse.

285
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:48,820
And we didn't talk about it.

286
00:22:51,939 --> 00:22:57,379
I couldn't put it into words then,
but I felt responsible.

287
00:22:59,580 --> 00:23:00,619
Responsible how?

288
00:23:06,820 --> 00:23:09,659
I wanted so much to be a mother...

289
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:14,379
but I just didn't think
that I was programmed that way.

290
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:18,859
That I was destined for something else.

291
00:23:18,939 --> 00:23:23,379
Ever since I was a kid,
since the Cortexiphan trials, I just...

292
00:23:24,340 --> 00:23:26,300
I was at odds.

293
00:23:26,939 --> 00:23:30,419
So how could I have
this incredible little girl?

294
00:23:38,619 --> 00:23:42,980
So when we lost her,
I felt like that was my punishment.

295
00:23:44,139 --> 00:23:49,899
My punishment for being too conflicted
to appreciate her when we had her.

296
00:23:57,179 --> 00:23:59,859
And that day at the restaurant...

297
00:24:01,020 --> 00:24:03,699
I knew
that I had to go back to New York...

298
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:06,500
because I didn't want to...

299
00:24:09,179 --> 00:24:10,740
find...

300
00:24:12,540 --> 00:24:15,060
what I knew we were going to find.

301
00:24:20,580 --> 00:24:21,740
I just...

302
00:24:21,820 --> 00:24:23,980
I just believed so strongly
that she was dead.

303
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:25,580
And I didn't want to see it.

304
00:24:25,659 --> 00:24:27,859
You thought I went back
because of my strength.

305
00:24:27,939 --> 00:24:29,740
She said that she admired me today.

306
00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:34,419
Olivia.

307
00:24:38,619 --> 00:24:40,780
I saw how you were with our little girl.

308
00:24:41,820 --> 00:24:44,139
I know how much you love her.

309
00:24:45,939 --> 00:24:50,060
And I'm not telling you to forget it,
but the past is the past.

310
00:24:50,980 --> 00:24:53,419
Don't let it get in the way of this.

311
00:24:53,859 --> 00:24:55,540
I don't know why it's happening...

312
00:24:55,619 --> 00:24:57,419
but our family got a second chance.

313
00:25:02,619 --> 00:25:04,580
So I'm gonna take it.

314
00:25:05,500 --> 00:25:07,100
Hey, guys.

315
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:11,179
We have a problem.
The Observers are tracking us.

316
00:25:19,419 --> 00:25:21,020
Hand me the seal.

317
00:25:24,060 --> 00:25:26,939
Walter, how long
until the suit is finished?

318
00:25:27,899 --> 00:25:29,899
I'm going as fast as I can. Why?

319
00:25:29,980 --> 00:25:32,340
The Observers know
that we're in the area.

320
00:25:32,419 --> 00:25:35,659
They saw us on the highway
when we stopped. Near route 11.

321
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:38,859
- You need to leave.
- I need one more material, copper.

322
00:25:38,939 --> 00:25:40,780
There's only three roads you could...

323
00:25:40,859 --> 00:25:44,020
Jonathan says there's
an encampment which trades metals.

324
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:45,580
No. You're not listening.

325
00:25:46,780 --> 00:25:49,100
We have until morning
before they find us.

326
00:25:49,179 --> 00:25:50,540
They'll destroy everything.

327
00:25:50,619 --> 00:25:52,859
All our work will be lost.
You need to go now.

328
00:25:52,939 --> 00:25:55,500
- If we can get down to that camp.
- Forget that camp.

329
00:25:55,580 --> 00:25:57,899
They won't trade
with anyone they don't know.

330
00:25:57,980 --> 00:26:00,780
You know them.
You've talked to them on your radio.

331
00:26:00,859 --> 00:26:02,379
You have contacts in that camp?

332
00:26:02,459 --> 00:26:06,260
I've communicated with them by radio.
But only when it suits them.

333
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:08,020
They don't even know who I am.

334
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:11,179
They don't want people going
into their quadrant, that I know.

335
00:26:11,260 --> 00:26:13,260
The way I see it,
you're our only chance.

336
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:15,020
They don't know us.

337
00:26:15,100 --> 00:26:16,939
Look, I'm armed. I'll go with you.

338
00:26:17,020 --> 00:26:19,659
- We can barter.
- Barter with what? What do you have?

339
00:26:19,740 --> 00:26:22,260
I've got some antibiotics, medicine.

340
00:26:22,340 --> 00:26:23,780
Go back to Boston.

341
00:26:23,859 --> 00:26:26,179
Make the suit there
and come back when it's safe.

342
00:26:26,260 --> 00:26:30,060
Baldies took away this Donald when
he went into that mine to get the rocks.

343
00:26:30,139 --> 00:26:34,699
They read you, find we're looking for the
same thing, they'll seal it permanently.

344
00:26:34,780 --> 00:26:38,699
You don't even know what the rocks
are for. You're putting all of us at risk.

345
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:15,820
I want to help you.

346
00:27:15,899 --> 00:27:17,260
I do.

347
00:27:20,859 --> 00:27:22,899
But what happens
if I don't come back?

348
00:27:24,540 --> 00:27:27,419
You may be used to facing death
all the time, but I'm not.

349
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:31,459
These are bad people.

350
00:27:32,219 --> 00:27:34,859
We may never make it back
from their camp.

351
00:27:36,379 --> 00:27:37,899
I have a lot to lose, you know.

352
00:27:39,340 --> 00:27:41,060
I know you do.

353
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:45,260
The rocks in that mine are important.

354
00:27:46,619 --> 00:27:48,659
And it's true,
we don't know what they do.

355
00:27:48,740 --> 00:27:51,020
But Walter will figure it out.

356
00:27:51,100 --> 00:27:54,899
And when he does, we're one step closer
to defeating the invaders.

357
00:28:06,179 --> 00:28:08,139
I'm not afraid to die.

358
00:28:10,899 --> 00:28:13,459
I just don't know
how to say goodbye to my son.

359
00:28:24,619 --> 00:28:26,060
All right.

360
00:28:27,899 --> 00:28:29,100
What do you need me to do?

361
00:28:30,340 --> 00:28:32,500
Get on your radio.
Tell them we're coming.

362
00:28:41,500 --> 00:28:45,740
Break, break, break.
Kilo-Alpha 42, come in.

363
00:28:45,820 --> 00:28:48,020
Looking to barter. Over.

364
00:28:50,060 --> 00:28:52,939
Kilo-Alpha 42.
Is anyone out there? Over.

365
00:28:53,020 --> 00:28:56,340
This is Kilo-Alpha. What's your business?

366
00:28:56,699 --> 00:28:58,459
I need copper.

367
00:28:58,980 --> 00:29:00,020
We don't have copper.

368
00:29:01,020 --> 00:29:02,699
Let me speak to Ivan.

369
00:29:25,500 --> 00:29:28,459
Tire tread. Matches the make and model
of the van they were in.

370
00:29:30,780 --> 00:29:32,060
Where does this road lead?

371
00:29:32,139 --> 00:29:34,419
Higher into the mountains.

372
00:30:05,179 --> 00:30:06,540
River?

373
00:30:16,540 --> 00:30:18,219
Look at me, son.

374
00:30:24,219 --> 00:30:26,780
What you saw back there in that tent...

375
00:30:29,980 --> 00:30:33,939
There's a lot more to the word "coward"...

376
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:37,459
and the word "hero" than you think.

377
00:30:37,780 --> 00:30:39,659
It's complicated.

378
00:30:40,540 --> 00:30:42,540
You're not a coward if you're frightened.

379
00:30:43,300 --> 00:30:45,419
That's not what it means.

380
00:30:46,820 --> 00:30:50,859
You're a coward
if you know what needs to be done...

381
00:30:50,939 --> 00:30:52,179
and you don't do it.

382
00:30:53,980 --> 00:30:57,580
I was telling that man out there
that I have a lot to lose.

383
00:30:58,780 --> 00:31:02,540
Our work, the life we have here.

384
00:31:03,500 --> 00:31:04,939
You.

385
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:09,740
I told him I love you so much,
I wouldn't know how to say goodbye.

386
00:31:14,939 --> 00:31:16,619
When your mother got sick...

387
00:31:17,179 --> 00:31:21,980
I promised her
I'd protect you no matter what.

388
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:24,619
But I'm conflicted.

389
00:31:26,980 --> 00:31:32,619
There's a time for recording history
and there's a time for making it.

390
00:31:34,300 --> 00:31:37,260
We don't get a lot of
opportunities up here to make it.

391
00:31:39,899 --> 00:31:42,300
I've decided to help them...

392
00:31:42,780 --> 00:31:44,659
to do my part.

393
00:31:47,619 --> 00:31:49,139
I don't wanna lose you.

394
00:31:49,219 --> 00:31:51,419
I don't wanna lose you, either.

395
00:31:52,699 --> 00:31:54,619
But protecting you...

396
00:31:54,699 --> 00:31:57,980
means making sure there's
a world out there that you can thrive in.

397
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:04,379
I love you so much.

398
00:32:07,340 --> 00:32:09,379
It's okay, Dad.

399
00:32:09,699 --> 00:32:11,100
I understand.

400
00:32:11,619 --> 00:32:15,020
No, you don't.

401
00:32:15,780 --> 00:32:17,740
But you will one day...

402
00:32:18,179 --> 00:32:20,340
when you have children of your own.

403
00:32:31,740 --> 00:32:35,980
The mineral you are looking for
is a crystallized form of quartz...

404
00:32:36,060 --> 00:32:39,419
that contains, in perfect proportion...

405
00:32:40,540 --> 00:32:46,060
will unlock its potential
and create a powerful energy source.

406
00:32:46,139 --> 00:32:51,540
I will explicitly outline
these refining methods in Tape 6.

407
00:32:54,379 --> 00:32:56,139
Or 7.

408
00:32:57,179 --> 00:32:58,939
Without this energy source...

409
00:32:59,260 --> 00:33:04,139
we will not be able to implement
the final phase of our plan.

410
00:33:04,699 --> 00:33:07,859
To my best calculations,
we'll need at least...

411
00:33:07,939 --> 00:33:10,459
18 kilograms of the crystals.

412
00:33:10,780 --> 00:33:15,060
That is about 40 pounds
in imperial measures.

413
00:33:16,580 --> 00:33:19,219
- Did you get all that?
- Yeah. Thanks, Astrid.

414
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:24,020
Hopefully, the suit will be strong enough
to bring up 40 pounds.

415
00:33:24,340 --> 00:33:25,340
Hey. Contact the camp?

416
00:33:25,659 --> 00:33:28,379
Yes. They found copper
and they're willing to trade.

417
00:33:28,699 --> 00:33:30,619
- How much copper?
- Two kilos.

418
00:33:30,939 --> 00:33:33,139
That's plenty. Too much even.

419
00:33:33,219 --> 00:33:35,100
Okay. Let's go.

420
00:33:36,619 --> 00:33:38,219
I can't.

421
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:48,300
You're looking for Ivan.

422
00:33:51,740 --> 00:33:54,419
You can give him these for the copper.

423
00:33:55,100 --> 00:33:57,300
They're schematics
for our solar technology.

424
00:33:58,619 --> 00:34:02,219
I'm not coming with you. I'm sorry.

425
00:34:06,179 --> 00:34:08,540
They'll trade for those. They're valuable.

426
00:34:09,699 --> 00:34:11,659
This will get you to the camp.

427
00:34:13,179 --> 00:34:14,540
Good luck.

428
00:34:17,619 --> 00:34:18,619
Don't worry about it.

429
00:34:19,339 --> 00:34:21,179
We'll be back soon.

430
00:34:22,819 --> 00:34:24,299
Wait.

431
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:28,540
Be careful.

432
00:34:46,619 --> 00:34:49,739
I don't understand.
This is exactly where he told us to go.

433
00:34:49,819 --> 00:34:53,619
- He said he knew where their camp was.
- Well, why would he send us here?

434
00:34:58,619 --> 00:35:01,219
Maybe he never wanted us
to find the camp.

435
00:35:17,219 --> 00:35:19,020
Edwin?

436
00:35:31,739 --> 00:35:33,420
What was that?

437
00:35:34,060 --> 00:35:35,819
It's the mine.

438
00:35:47,179 --> 00:35:49,219
There was no copper.

439
00:35:50,540 --> 00:35:52,699
When he called their camp,
they didn't have copper.

440
00:35:52,779 --> 00:35:56,060
It was the only way to get what you need.

441
00:36:31,699 --> 00:36:36,699
You got what you came for, now leave,
before the invaders find you here.

442
00:37:38,699 --> 00:37:40,580
A hero died today...

443
00:37:41,420 --> 00:37:44,739
sacrificing himself in order to get
the Fringe team...

444
00:37:44,819 --> 00:37:48,060
resources they need to save our world.

445
00:37:55,659 --> 00:38:02,020
Edwin Massey, the recorder of history,
made history.

446
00:38:24,980 --> 00:38:28,299
- Where did you get this vehicle?
- I found it on the side of the road.

447
00:38:28,380 --> 00:38:32,060
The keys were in the ignition.
I didn't steal it.

448
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:36,779
He will be greatly missed.

449
00:38:41,460 --> 00:38:43,580
Like other great men before him...

450
00:38:43,659 --> 00:38:48,699
he gave his life selflessly
in pursuit of freedom...

451
00:38:52,139 --> 00:38:54,460
and a better future.

452
00:39:16,179 --> 00:39:18,940
Now, this is a ride.

